# Python bytecode & cache
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Local Virtual Environments & Secrets
.env
.venv/
env/
venv/

# Data directory contents (keeps the folders via .gitkeep, but ignores actual data files)
data/raw/*
data/processed/*
data/archive/*
!data/**/.gitkeep

# IDE configuration files
.vscode/
.idea/
tempCodeRunnerFile.py


# __pycache__/: Python automatically creates hidden folders named __pycache__ to store compiled bytecode so your scripts run faster. You don't need to upload these cache files to GitHub because Python will just recreate them locally on your teammates' computers.
# *.py[cod]: Ignores compiled Python files (.pyc, .pyo, .pyd) for the exact same reason.
# .pytest_cache/ & .ruff_cache/: Ignores temporary cache files generated by testing (pytest) and code formatting (ruff) tools.
# .env: (Critical Security) This file holds your private database passwords and API keys. If you don't ignore this, your passwords will become public on GitHub for anyone to see.
# .venv/, env/, venv/: These are your local Python virtual environments. They contain hundreds of installed library files that take up gigabytes of space. Every team member creates their own virtual environment locally, so you never upload this folder to GitHub.
# data/raw/*, data/processed/*, data/archive/*: Tells Git to ignore any large CSV, JSON, or database files placed inside your data folders so your repository doesn't get overloaded with heavy data.
# !data/**/.gitkeep: The exclamation mark means "except". This tells Git: Ignore all the heavy data files inside these folders, but do keep the empty folders alive using the tiny .gitkeep files so your teammates have the exact same folder structure when they clone the project.
# .vscode/: Ignores local Visual Studio Code settings and workspace configurations that belong only to your computer setup.
# .idea/: Ignores configuration files for PyCharm or other JetBrains editors, just in case someone on another team or a future project uses them.
# tempCodeRunnerFile.py: A temporary file that VS Code's Code Runner extension automatically creates when you run partial code snippets. Ignoring it keeps your folder clean.
